{"slug": "time-of-flight-sensor-working", "targets": ["time of flight sensor"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">Time of Flight Sensor Timing Quick Reference Table</h2><p><strong>Time of flight sensor</strong> electronics must resolve extremely short times, because light travels about 0.3 m per nanosecond and the pulse covers the distance twice. Using d = c × t ÷ 2 with c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s, the table shows the round trip time for typical target distances.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Target distance</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Round trip time</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Typical sensor class</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1 cm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.067 ns (67 ps)</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Proximity and gesture</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10 cm</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0.67 ns</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Short range module</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1 m</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">6.67 ns</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Robot obstacle detection</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">4 m</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">26.7 ns</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Long range indoor module</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100 m</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">667 ns</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Lidar scanner</td></tr></table></div><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">Phase Method: Modulation Frequency vs Unambiguous Range</h3><p>Indirect sensors measure phase shift instead of time, with d = c × φ ÷ (4π × f). The unambiguous range is c ÷ (2 × f), so a higher modulation frequency gives finer resolution but a shorter range before the phase wraps.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Modulation frequency</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Unambiguous range</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">10 MHz</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">15 m</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">20 MHz</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">7.5 m</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">50 MHz</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">3 m</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100 MHz</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">1.5 m</td></tr></table></div><p>A 1 mm distance resolution therefore needs about 6.7 ps of timing resolution, which is why direct sensors average many SPAD histogram returns rather than timing one pulse.</p>"}